      cat: "fcc",
      a: "No. It was abandoned in 1987 and the last pieces were struck down in 2000. There is no legal duty to give equal time to opposing views on an issue.",
      detail: "This one comes up constantly, usually from a listener who is unhappy, and it is worth being able to answer confidently. The Fairness Doctrine required broadcasters to cover controversial issues and to present contrasting views. The FCC stopped enforcing it in 1987 and formally removed the last language in 2011. The related personal attack and political editorial rules were struck down in 2000.\n\nWhat does still exist is the equal opportunities rule, and that applies only to legally qualified candidates for office, not to issues or opinions. See the elections section.\n\nNone of which means we should be one-sided. It means the obligation is editorial and ours, not legal and enforced."
